use javax.swing.JFrame
use java.awt.GraphicsEnvironment

# The display-backed half of C13, run only under a display the harness itself provides.
#
# `c13_display` asserts the *relationship* between headless detection and frame realisation, so
# its single recorded expectation is correct on a desktop and on a bare runner alike. That is
# what makes it host-independent, and it is also why it cannot prove display-backed coverage: on
# a host with no display its `worked` branch is unreachable, and `headless == not worked` is
# satisfied by `true` / `false` alone. A green run there is evidence about the invariant, not
# about a window being mapped.
#
# This probe is the opposite: it *requires* a display and fails without one, so it can only pass
# where the display-backed path actually ran. The runner launches it under Xvfb, which picks a
# free display number and exports `DISPLAY`, and the harness refuses to run this probe at all
# when `DISPLAY` is unset on Linux, because a silent fallback to headless would turn a green line
# into a false claim.
#
# The three printed lines are deliberately about observable state, not about policy:
#
#   `headless` must be `false`, which is the check that the JVM accepted the display rather than
#   quietly switching to headless mode.
#   `displayable` must be `true` after `setVisible`, which is the window being realised by the
#   window system rather than merely allocated.
#   `closed` must be `true` after `dispose`, so the probe leaves nothing behind on a shared
#   virtual display and the successful realisation is shown to be reversible.
#
# What this still does not prove is how a *window manager* composites the window. Xvfb maps the
# window and reports it displayable; it runs no compositor. That remains a desktop property.
print GraphicsEnvironment.isHeadless()

frame = JFrame("c13-mapped")
frame.setSize(80, 60)
frame.setVisible(true)
print frame.isDisplayable()
frame.dispose()
print frame.isDisplayable() == false
